(a) The terms and conditions applicable to any franchise or revocable license granted pursuant to this article shall be set forth in the separate ordinance granting the franchise or revocable license or in a separate written agreement. Such separate ordinance or written agreement, among other things, may address the following subjects, to the extent not prohibited by law:
(1) The term of the franchise or revocable license, provided, however, that the initial term of the revocable license shall not exceed five (5) years and shall not be renewed for terms exceeding five (5) years;
(2) The franchise area authorized by the franchise, or the streets that may be used pursuant to the revocable license;
(3) The compensation to be paid to the city;
(4) The circumstances upon which the franchise or revocable license may be terminated or canceled;
(5) The mechanisms, such as performance bonds, security funds or letters of credit, to be put in place to ensure the performance of the grantee's obligations under the franchise or revocable license;
(6) For the limited purpose of ensuring that the grantee is complying with the terms of the franchise or revocable license, including, but not limited to, any compensation or street management provisions, the city's right to inspect the facilities and records of the grantee;
(7) Insurance and indemnification requirements applicable to the grantee;
(8) The obligation of the grantee to maintain complete and accurate books of account and records with respect to its obligations under the franchise or revocable license, including, but not limited to, any compensation or street management provisions, and the city's inspection rights with respect thereto;
(9) Provisions to ensure quality workmanship and construction methods in the streets;
(10) Provisions to ensure that the grantee will comply with all applicable city, state and federal laws, regulations, rules and policies;
(11) Provisions to ensure adequate oversight by the city of the grantee's use of streets and compliance with the franchise or revocable license;
(12) Provisions relating to an assignment or other transfer of the franchise or revocable license or the telecommunications system that results in a change in control;
(13) Remedies available to the city to protect the city's interest in the event of the grantee's failure to comply with terms and conditions of the franchise or revocable license;
(14) Provisions to ensure that the grantee will obtain all licenses and permits necessary to provide telecommunications services by use of the city's streets from any governmental body having jurisdiction over the grantee, including, but not limited to, the Federal Communications Commission;
(15) Provisions to ensure that the grantee will protect the property of the city from damage or interruption of city operations resulting from the construction, operation, maintenance, repair or removal of improvements of the telecommunications system, or from the provision of facility services;
(16) Provisions designed to minimize the extent to which the public use of the streets of the city are disrupted in connection with the construction of improvements relating to the telecommunications system, or the provision of facility services; and
(17) Such other provisions as the city reasonably determines are necessary or appropriate to permit the city to manage its streets or ensure compliance with the franchise or revocable license, to the extent not prohibited by law.
(b) The city administrator shall make available to an applicant a model franchise or revocable license, as applicable, containing the terms and conditions the city seeks to impose pursuant to section 7-116.
(Ord. No. 01-07, § 1, 11-5-01)

393:
(a) Generally; exceptions. No person shall cause, suffer, allow or permit open burning in any area of the city except as follows:
(1) The area to be burned shall be no closer than one hundred fifty (150) feet of any structure, residential, commercial or otherwise;
(2) No more than one (1) pile per site no larger than six (6) feet by six (6) feet;
(3) Destruction of combustible demolition or construction materials either on site or transported to a burning facility approved by the chief or transported to a burning facility approved by the chief of fire department unless prohibited by local ordinance and/or regulation;
(4) The burning-over of any forestland by the owners of such land;
(5) For recreational purposes of cooking food for immediate home consumption, with the following exceptions:
a. Open-flame cooking devices. Charcoal burners and other open-flame cooking devices shall not be operated on combustible balconies or within ten (10) feet of combustible construction. This exception shall not apply to one- and two-family dwellings.
b. Liquefied petroleum gas-fueled cooking devices. LP-gas burners having an LP-gas container with a water capacity greater than 2.5 pounds (nominal one (1) pound LP-gas capacity) shall not be located on combustible balconies or within ten (10) feet of combustible construction. This exception shall not apply to one- and two-family dwellings.
(6) Fires set for purposes of training firefighting personnel when authorized by the appropriate governmental entity and the guidelines set forth by the chief of fire department are strictly observed;
(7) Disposal of tree limbs from storm damage;
(8) For weed abatement, disease and pest prevention;
(9) Operation of devices using open flames, such as tar kettles, blow torches, welding torches, portable heaters and other flame-making equipment;
(10) Setting and maintenance by contractors and tradesmen of miscellaneous small fires necessary to such activities as street paving work installation or repair of utilities provided that such fires are kept small in size, no smoke emissions exceed forty (40) percent opacity and that local ordinances and regulations do not prohibit such actions;
(11) Open burning in other than predominately residential areas for the purpose of land clearing or construction of right-of-way maintenance provided the following conditions are met:
a. Prevailing winds at the time of the burning are away from the major portion of the area's population.
b. The location of the burning is at least one thousand (1,000) feet from any dwelling location in a predominantly residential area.
c. The amount of dirt on or in the material being burned is minimized.
d. Heavy oils, asphaltic materials, items containing natural or synthetic rubber or any materials other than plant growth are not being burned.
e. No more than one (1) pile sixty (60) feet by sixty (60) feet or equivalent is being burned within a nine-acre area at one (1) time.
(12) Disposal of all packaging materials previously containing explosives in accordance with U.S. Department of Labor Safety Regulations;
(13) Pits to be utilized for burning, no more than one (1) pit per site, with a maximum dimension of thirty (30) feet in length, thirty (30) feet in width and eight (8) feet in depth;
(14) For pits being used for burning material, an air curtain destructor shall be used at all times during burning;
(15) All burning shall begin no earlier than 9:00 a.m., and said burning shall cease at 6:00 p.m. the same day. At 6:00 p.m., all fires will be extinguished, and there will be no overnight burning under any circumstances;
(16) All open burning shall be attended by a competent person at all times. The person responsible shall provide whatever is necessary for the extinguishment of fires at all times;
(17) The chief of the fire department shall have the authority to order no open burning due to conditions prevalent.
(b) Conditions to exceptions. In those counties whose total populations as listed in the latest census exceeds sixty-five thousand (65,000), the only legal exceptions to the general prohibition against open burning shall be exceptions (a)(1), (2), (4) through (6), (9), (10) and (12) above; provided, however, that such burning, whenever feasible, shall be conducted between 10:00 a.m. and 6:00 p.m. If adequate disposal facilities for the particular combustible materials involved are not reasonably available, the other items under subsection (a) shall also be permitted except that in no event shall the open burning of more than one hundred (100) cubic yards per day of material described in exception (11) of subsection (a) be permitted unless the person performing such burning shall have first given the director two (2) days' written notice of the time and place of such burning.
(c) General prohibition. Beginning January 1, 1996, all open burning shall be prohibited during the months of May, June, July, August and September. The only exceptions to the general prohibition against open burning during the months of May, June, July, August and September shall be:
(1) For the recreational purpose of cooking food for human consumption;
(2) Fires set for the purpose of training firefighting personnel when authorized by the appropriate governmental entity and the guidelines set forth by the chief of the fire department are strictly observed;
(3) Operation of devices using open flames such as tar kettles, blow torches, welding torches, portable heaters, and other flame-making equipment where approved safety measures are used;
(4) Setting and maintenance by contractors and tradesmen of miscellaneous small warming fires for their workers; and
(5) Carrying out recognized agricultural procedures necessary for production and harvesting of crops with the appropriate forestry permit.
(Ord. No. 90-10, 8-6-90; Ord. No. 96-03, § 1, 6-3-96; Ord. No. 09-03, §§ 3—5, 3-2-09)

